            <ArticleTitle>Beta-blockers in left ventricular systolic dysfunction--from evidence to practice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>23-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Five percent of all hospital medical admissions are patients with heart failure. The incidence is about one new case per 1000 of the general population per year, increasing to &gt;10 per 1000 in those aged &gt;or=85 years. Although the evidence that beta-blockers reduce mortality by about 36% when added to angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors is overwhelming, clinicians are still reluctant to use beta-blockers in heart failure, especially in older patients. Here, we examine the evidence for the use of beta-blockers in heart failure in older people and explore the practicalities of their use.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
